{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' prompts/refactor.md\"","aggregated_output":"You are refactoring one Lean 4 proof for the Lean Refactor Arena.\n\n## Target\n\n- Theorem: `{name}`\n- Corpus: {source}\n- Work file (edit this file, nothing else): `{work_file}`\n- Lean project root: `{project_dir}` (toolchain {primary_version})\n- Must also compile unchanged on: {other_versions}\n\nThe work file is the prefix of the upstream source file (imports, `open`s,\n`variable`s, section context) followed by the target declaration with its\noriginal proof. Everything above the declaration is context: do not touch it.\nScoring re-splices your declaration into the pristine upstream file, so any edit\nyou make above the declaration is discarded — only the declaration counts.\n\n## Official objective\n\nMaximize the arena leaderboard score, not length or heartbeats separately:\n\n`combined = (length_reduction_pct + heartbeat_reduction_pct + survival_pct) / 3`\n\nFull survival on every listed toolchain is a hard constraint. Among candidates\nthat survive everywhere, rank by:\n\n`objective_sum_pct = length_reduction_pct + heartbeat_reduction_pct`\n\nThis is a real tradeoff, and the sum is the only tiebreak. A longer proof wins\nwhen its heartbeat gain exceeds its length loss; over-golfing tokens loses if\nelaboration gets sufficiently more expensive. Compare the sum printed by\n`check` rather than reasoning about it.\n\nReferences for this problem: {ref_heartbeats} heartbeats and {ref_length} proof\ntokens.\n\n## How to work\n\nYou have roughly 30 minutes of wall clock and no cap on tool calls or checks.\nUse them. Do not stop at the first compiling improvement; big score jumps\nusually come from a second or third structural rewrite, not from polishing the\nfirst candidate.\n\nTwo feedback loops, and using the right one for each purpose matters:\n\n```\ncd {project_dir} && lake env lean Arena/{work_file_name}   # fast, errors only, no score\ncd {competition_dir} && python3 arena.py check --name \"{name}\"                 # newest toolchain + score\ncd {competition_dir} && python3 arena.py check --name \"{name}\" --all-versions  # + survival across toolchains\n```\n\nIterate on *compile errors* with `lake env lean` — it is cheaper and you can\nrun it as often as you like. Use `check` when you want to know whether a\ncompiling candidate is actually better, and `--all-versions` to confirm a\ncandidate you intend to submit. `check` is the scorer, and its heartbeat number\nis the one that counts; do not try to measure heartbeats yourself.\n\nUse `rg`/`sed` on the corpus under `.lake/packages` freely — finding the right\nexisting lemma is usually what produces a large win. Do not use Lean MCP tools.\n\nKeep a copy of your best compiling candidate (a comment block, or just a note\nof the text) before attempting a bigger rewrite, so a failed experiment never\ncosts you a result you already had.\n\n## Strategies that tend to work\n\n**Big wins come from changing the proof's structure, not from golfing the\nexisting structure.** Token-level cleanup is worth doing after a structural\nmove, not instead of one.\n\n### Change what you induct on\n\nIf the original inducts on a term (`induction e`) and then, in every case,\nre-derives a well-formedness / normalization / typing hypothesis with\n`simp_all`, try inducting on that hypothesis instead. The induction\nhypotheses already carry the property, and impossible constructors become\n`nofun`:\n\n```\n-  intro hP\n-  induction e <;> simp_all\n+  intro hP\n+  induction hP with\n+  | empty => exact fun _ => nofun\n+  | step _ ih => exact ih\n```\n\n### Factor repetition into a local combinator\n\nYou cannot add a helper `lemma`/`def`. You *can* bind a small combinator\ninside the proof with `have`/`let` and reuse it. If the same two-line\n`Subset`/`And`/`Iff` plumbing appears in several cases, pull it out once.\n\n### Find the lemma that removes the induction\n\nIf a proof inducts over a derivation (updates, reductions, judgments), search\nthe surrounding module for a lemma that already states the result —\ncommutation, \"updated\", monotonicity, inversion. Collapsing an induction into\n`refine` plus a few explicit `rw`s is often the largest heartbeat cut\navailable. A failed first attempt at using such a lemma is not a reason to\ngive up on it; retry with explicit binders.\n\n### Collapse witness-then-prove, and drop automation\n\nMechanical, cheap rewrites:\n\n- `use w` then `exact ⟨p, q⟩` → `exact ⟨_, p, q⟩` (let `_` infer the witness)\n- `rw [h]` where `h` is an equality → `cases h` (substitutes without rewrite)\n- `.ctor a b` → `.ctor ..` when the arguments are obvious\n- Replace `grind` / `aesop` / bare `simp_all` with the constructor or lemma\n  they found. Those tactics are usually the heartbeat hog and a survival risk\n  across toolchains.\n\n### Small golf, cheapest first\n\n- `by exact t` → `t`; `⟨rfl, rfl⟩` for one-constructor goals; `h.1`/`.symm`/`.mp`\n  chains; `h ▸ p` for transport; `rwa`; `nofun` for impossible cases.\n- `simp` → `simp only [named lemmas]`, or better, an explicit `rw`/`exact`.\n- Fill stray `_` explicitly when it is an expensive unification, not a cheap one.\n- Prefer old, stable lemma names over freshly added ones — a name that gets\n  renamed across the toolchain window costs you the survival third. Use `exact?`\n  to *discover* a term if you want, then inline the term, not the call.\n\n## Reading the score\n\n- A compiling candidate with a small `objective_sum_pct` often means the\n  original proof's shape is still there. Search the corpus and reconsider the\n  induction target before token-golfing.\n- Heartbeats stuck near the reference: automation is still doing the work\n  somewhere. Find it and make it explicit.\n- Survival failure on an older toolchain: that is a name or normal-form\n  dependency, not a real proof failure. Repair it explicitly rather than\n  reverting to automation.\n- A strategy that failed to compile is not a strategy that was wrong. If an\n  idea looked right but the implementation broke, retry it with explicit\n  constructor binders or a local `have` combinator before abandoning it.\n\n## Hard rules\n\nThese come from the competition's upload validator, not from us. Everything\nelse about how you work is your call.\n\n- The statement must stay **byte-identical**, including binders and\n  implicit/instance arguments. Only the proof after `:=` may change.\n- The submission is *the statement plus one proof, nothing else*. Do **not**\n  add any top-level declaration: no `lemma`/`theorem` helpers, no `def`,\n  `abbrev`, `instance`, `structure`, `inductive`, `class`, `opaque`,\n  `attribute`, `macro`, `notation`, `syntax`, `deriving instance`, and no new\n  top-level `set_option ... in` / `open ... in` beyond whatever the statement\n  already carries. Factor shared work into `have`/`let`/`suffices` **inside**\n  the proof.\n- Forbidden anywhere in the proof: `sorry`, `admit`, `axiom`, `native_decide`,\n  `ofReduceBool`, `ofReduceNat`, `#eval`, `#reduce`, `#exit`, `IO.*`,\n  `unsafe`, `extern`, `initialize`, `run_cmd`, `run_elab`,\n  `#count_heartbeats`, `@[implemented_by]`, `@[extern]`.\n- No `set_option maxHeartbeats` to make something fit; the metric is a counter\n  delta, so raising the limit scores nothing and hurts survival.\n- Do not edit any other file, do not edit anything under `.lake/`, and do not\n  change the project's toolchain or dependencies.\n\n## Optional notes\n\n`{competition_dir}/STRATEGY.md` has the long playbook (per-metric rankings with\nmeasured magnitudes from the Lean community, robustness traps, why each\nconstruct is banned); `LEAN_SYNTAX.md` and `VERSION_MATRIX.md` cover syntax\nand per-toolchain differences. Open a section when it is relevant — searching\nthe corpus for lemmas is usually more valuable than reading these front to back.\n\nDo not treat `{competition_dir}/runs/` or `{competition_dir}/traces/` as\nhints. Those are prior attempts on this same warm-up set; using them is\ncheating the local A/B, and they will not exist for hidden leaderboard\nproblems.\n\n## When you are done\n\nLeave your best proof in the work file as the last declaration, confirmed by an\n`--all-versions` check. Reply with: what you changed, final length and\nheartbeats versus the reference, the `objective_sum_pct` you reached, which\ntoolchains pass, and which rewrites you tried that did not work.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
